course: dictionary
lesson: 5438
title: Loud sorrow in words
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A lamentation is a loud cry of grief, or a sad piece of writing that mourns a loss. Read the clues around the word: loss, sorrow and crying all point to its meaning. «Let us examine our ways and test them, and let us return to the Lord» is a sorrowful, prayer-like kind of writing.
explain: The word is formal and old-fashioned, so you will meet it in stories, poems and the Bible. Its everyday partners are crying and wailing. If you meet it, ask: what word would fit here? Try crying in the sentence and see if it still makes sense.
explain: Lamentation is a noun. It can be countable (a lamentation, lamentations) or uncountable (lamentation filled the hall).
fact: A lamentation is a loud cry of grief, or a sad piece of writing about a loss.
fact: Lamentations is also a book of the Bible, made of sad poems about the fall of Jerusalem.
fact: «Let us examine our ways and test them» comes from Lamentations 3:40.
fact: Ignatius of Loyola, in the 1500s, taught a daily prayer called the Examen.
fact: Everyday words for lamentation are crying and wailing.
exercise: «Let us examine our ways and test them, and let us return to the Lord» is from Lamentations 3:40. What is Lamentations? | *A book of the Bible with sad poems | A cookery book from the 1500s | A type of loud drum | A kind of happy song
exercise: Which everyday word is closest in meaning to lamentation? | sleeping | *wailing | whispering | laughing
exercise: Is lamentation a formal or a casual word? | Only used in shopping lists | A word for a type of food | *Formal and old-fashioned | Slang from the playground
test: What does lamentation mean? | *Loud crying and sad words because of grief or loss | A happy party with music | A short and polite reply | A quiet, sleepy afternoon
test: What did Ignatius of Loyola teach in the 1500s? | A type of castle | A new kind of ship | A famous painting | *The daily prayer called the Examen
exam: Mia hears loud sobbing and sad words from the next room and calls it a lamentation. What does she mean? | The room is empty and silent | Everyone is celebrating | A happy song | *Someone is crying loudly with grief
